WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:03.520
I have an entire operation
around shopping.

00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:05.720
I take it very, very seriously.

00:00:05.800 --> 00:00:08.320
I've got loads of items
that I just never even wear.

00:00:08.840 --> 00:00:11.920
Beautiful, beautiful quality.
I've had it for over a year.

00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:14.720
It hasn't even been
taken off the hanger.

00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:20.800
When we look around
the high street and stores

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:22.800
we see lots and lots of stuff,

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:25.520
but the most interesting thing
about consumption

00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:28.440
and buying is what's
going on in our minds.

00:00:28.520 --> 00:00:31.720
The story of stuff
begins in our imagination

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:33.280
and in our fantasies.

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:36.560
That's the space where we
dream about all the things

00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:38.640
that we would really,
really love to have

00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:40.800
and what a wonderful life
we would have,

00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:42.920
if only we could have this stuff.

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:45.920
I always think, "Yep, this is it.
This is the item.

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:48.440
This is what's going
to make me happy."

00:00:48.520 --> 00:00:52.040
What we consume
is an expression of our identity,

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:56.480
because our identity consists
of a number of different facets.

00:00:56.680 --> 00:00:59.520
We have an actual self, who we
actually are most of the time,

00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:02.040
but then we also have a social self.

00:01:02.120 --> 00:01:06.040
We buy products, clothes that express

00:01:06.120 --> 00:01:08.240
who we want to be
at any point in time.

00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:09.440
I have two moods.

00:01:09.520 --> 00:01:12.120
So, I have this mood.

00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:16.000
And I also have this mood.

00:01:16.440 --> 00:01:20.880
And they're both as vital to me
as the other.

00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:23.920
Like, I feel like sometimes clothes
help me take on a character.

00:01:24.880 --> 00:01:29.560
Buying something new
makes me feel excited

00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:31.280
and like, full of adrenaline.

00:01:31.480 --> 00:01:33.200
Marketers are very, very good

00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:36.600
at speaking to the
parasympathetic nervous system,

00:01:36.680 --> 00:01:39.080
the one that makes
your heart just flutter,

00:01:39.160 --> 00:01:41.080
they can't do anything about it.

00:01:41.160 --> 00:01:43.520
I buy clothes quite frequently.

00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:45.600
I tend to do a lot
of my shopping online

00:01:45.880 --> 00:01:49.840
so I have orders come in
at least once or twice a week.

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:52.400
I actually have an order right
now that I'm going to pick up.

00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:55.760
It's a new dress and
I'm quite excited to wear it.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:03.760
Excessive consumption
is actually proven to make us feel

00:02:04.280 --> 00:02:06.720
if not bad, it doesn't
make us feel good.

00:02:06.800 --> 00:02:09.200
Recent research has shown that

00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:11.640
when we have too many things

00:02:11.880 --> 00:02:13.640
we are constantly dissatisfied.

00:02:13.840 --> 00:02:17.240
Let's say I spend about half
my lunch break looking at clothes,

00:02:17.320 --> 00:02:19.360
and then I look a bit
in between work tasks.

00:02:20.280 --> 00:02:22.000
And then you add that to
some time when I get home,

00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:23.320
and then the actual purchasing -

00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:25.840
I would say maybe like
three hours a day

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:32.880
is spent looking at websites,
looking at baskets, returning things,

00:02:32.960 --> 00:02:35.000
yeah, it takes up
quite a lot of my time.

00:02:38.240 --> 00:02:40.560
Before the 16th, 17th Century

00:02:40.640 --> 00:02:47.200
many cultures thought of things
as opposed to the self or the soul,

00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:50.320
and what we see in the
modern period is a reversal.

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:54.120
It's a cultural shift that
things start to be seen

00:02:54.480 --> 00:02:56.840
as central to our identity.

00:03:00.880 --> 00:03:03.800
There's something really interesting
happening actually with consumers.

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:06.960
People are actually beginning
to get fed up with stuff.

00:03:07.040 --> 00:03:11.680
We spoke to three generations of
grandmothers, mothers and daughters.

00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:15.160
For the grandmother generation,
in that post-war era,

00:03:15.240 --> 00:03:19.280
there was a much greater emphasis
placed upon being frugal.

00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:22.040
The mother's generation,
they wanted to be modern

00:03:22.120 --> 00:03:25.920
and embrace technologies
like the freezer and the microwave.

00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:29.360
The daughters rejected a lot of that.

00:03:29.440 --> 00:03:32.560
They saw themselves
as being experimental,

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:37.200
buying different ethnic foods,
trying different styles of cooking,

00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:40.360
buying organic produce,
ethical products.

00:03:40.440 --> 00:03:43.720
What we found was that these
generations of women

00:03:43.960 --> 00:03:46.800
were all the same
in wanting to be different,

00:03:46.880 --> 00:03:48.440
and the way in which they did that

00:03:48.520 --> 00:03:51.800
was through how they shopped
and bought and used stuff.

00:03:51.880 --> 00:03:54.920
With the topic around climate change

00:03:55.000 --> 00:03:58.560
and the impact the fashion
industry has on the climate,

00:03:58.880 --> 00:04:00.640
I am trying to be more
mindful of my shopping.

00:04:00.720 --> 00:04:04.360
So I've recently made a rule
to try and make sure that

00:04:04.440 --> 00:04:07.440
at least 50% of my wardrobe
comes from vintage stores,

00:04:07.520 --> 00:04:10.440
and I really want to get to a point
where I'm more mindful about it

00:04:10.520 --> 00:04:14.440
and it's less fast fashion
and less vapid.

00:04:14.680 --> 00:04:18.640
We've got so much stuff now, we
don't really know what to do with it.

00:04:18.840 --> 00:04:20.320
We spend a lot of time
trying to work out

00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:22.680
how to get rid of the stuff
that we've got.

00:04:23.520 --> 00:04:25.120
Now, rationally we might say,

00:04:25.200 --> 00:04:27.600
{\an2}"Well maybe we should
stop buying all of this stuff."

00:04:27.680 --> 00:04:28.920
But we don't do that.

00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:32.120
What we do is retreat
back into our imaginations

00:04:32.200 --> 00:04:37.600
and into our fantasies and so
we start the cycle all over again.

00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:42.480
I would say yes, I do have a mild
problem when it comes to shopping.

00:04:44.120 --> 00:04:47.760
But I like to think that
I could stop if I wanted to.

00:04:48.960 --> 00:04:51.280
Oh my god, that's exactly
what an addict says, isn't it?

